   "statement": "Moreover, these maps must satisfy the following universal property: for any maps $f \\colon S \\rightarrow X$ and $g \\colon T \\rightarrow X$, there must be a unique map $h \\colon S + T \\rightarrow X$ making this diagram commute: $$\\dgARROWLENGTH=0.5\\dgARROWLENGTH \\begin{diagram}[S + T] \\node{S} \\arrow{se,b}{i} \\arrow{ese,t}{f} \\\\ \\node[2]{S+T} \\arrow{e,t}{h} \\node{X} \\\\ \\node{T} \\arrow{ne,t}{j} \\arrow{ene,b}{g} \\end{diagram}$$ In other words, a map from a disjoint union of $S$ and $T$ to some set contains the same information as a pair of maps from $S$ and $T$ to that set.",
